    url: 'using-start.html#1-start-with-a-framework-and-a-scope',
    text: ' A framework is the catalogue of controls you assess against. Choose one in the framework switcher in the navigation bar before you look at controls, clauses, coverage or the Statement of Applicability. The choice follows you through the application. Switching framework shows the same evidence and risks through a different catalogue.  Most people begin with the KEEN Assurance Framework (KEEN-AF:1.0) , a practical set of controls that works with the reusable risk library. Treat it as a starting point for your own programme.  Scope comes next. Agree which parts of the organisation, which assets, which people and which frameworks are in play. For each relevant control, record why it is in or out of scope and link the policies, risks and evidence that support that decision.  Going deeper: where KEEN-AF comes from KEEN-AF is based on Vanta’s controls , licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0 (Copyright 2021 Vanta Inc). KEEN’s adaptations cover control organisation, identifiers and consolidation of duplicates. KEEN itself is also licensed under Apache 2.0. Certification and audit conclusions need their own assessment process. When you apply an external standard, consult its licensed text.  A practical first session  Sign in and select the framework you intend to assess. Open Controls and the Statement of Applicability , and Clauses when the framework has them. Add your assets and responsible people under ISMS . Create one asset-specific risk, directly or from the reusable library. Ask an administrator to configure an evidence definition for one operational source. Open a collected event, its artefact and its mapped controls. Create an audit and sample that event, with a note on what it demonstrates. ',
