    text: ' The framework editor: the node being edited is highlighted in the tree while its reference, title, parent and scope are changed on the right.  Administrators maintain frameworks in Admin → Frameworks . Everyday work there is small: pick a framework, adjust a node, save.  Choose Select framework to edit an existing framework, or New framework to create one. Enter a slug, name, version, description and optional upstream URL, then Save framework . Choose New node to add a Clause, Control or Custom node. Give it a reference and title, then a description, an optional parent of the same category, an upstream URL, a sort order and its In scope setting. A control can also link to related clauses. Choose Save node . To edit a node, select it in the searchable tree. The node you are editing is marked in the tree and stays marked after you save.  Identities stay put. A slug, a reference and a category identify a framework or node across the whole application, so the editor does not rename them. To change one, create the replacement and move the relationships deliberately.  Going deeper: deleting nodes and licensed text Deleting a node is blocked while it has child nodes, clause links or evidence mappings. Review those relationships first. When history must survive, mark the node out of scope instead of deleting it. Use upstream links to point at authorised guidance. Paste licensed standard text only when your organisation has the right to store and share it. ',
