    text: ' The same event can support requirements in several catalogues. Two mechanisms make that happen:  A mapping rule can target controls or clauses in more than one framework. An administrator can approve a directed cross-framework control relationship and record why two requirements overlap. The receiving control then shows evidence from the linked source control.  When you assess a receiving requirement, read both the relationship’s rationale and the underlying evidence.  Going deeper: the catalogue and its licences KEEN includes 94 frameworks: 88 catalogues from Open Security Architecture (OSA) , plus the KEEN Assurance Framework, UK Digital Verification Services Trust Framework, Cyber Essentials, Risk Ledger, ISO 9001:2015 (including Amendment 1:2024) and ISO 9001:2026. The OSA catalogues include ISO/IEC 27001:2022, SOC 2 and the HIPAA Security Rule. Administrators choose the enabled frameworks in Admin → Frameworks; KEEN Assurance is enabled by default. OSA-derived catalogue data and KEEN’s adaptations are licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0) , Copyright Open Security Architecture and contributors. KEEN keeps source links and licence metadata on the controls. Adaptations include database identifiers, hierarchy, display titles, combined descriptions and relationships derived from shared NIST references. When you redistribute or adapt this data, retain attribution, identify changes and follow the ShareAlike terms; see OSA’s licence terms . KEEN’s software is licensed separately under Apache 2.0. ',
