    text: ' A careful first rollout saves time later. This is the checklist administrators follow for a new adapter.  Define a source: choose the type, name it, enter its address and credentials, and save. Credentials are encrypted on the server.  Choose one adapter and one small upstream input. Confirm outbound connectivity and TLS validation from the runtime, and grant a dedicated upstream identity access to that input only. Set the adapter’s enable flag and connection credentials in the server environment, then restart the affected services according to your deployment process. Create the collection input and evidence definition in Admin → Sources & Evidence Mapping , and run the adapter from Admin → Imports & adapters . Open the resulting event and artefact. Verify timestamp, source, system, actor, action, outcome and collection identity. A run that returns zero events may simply have found no new data. Preview the definition against one positive and one negative example. Confirm the intended controls exist in the chosen framework and that other inputs cannot match by accident. Enable routine scheduling and watch the next run. Note the expected lookback, the upstream quota and what a failure looks like.  Adapter-specific settings for Loki, CloudWatch Logs, GitHub, Forgejo, Jenkins, Taiga, BookStack, RSS and Google Workspace are in Administering KEEN . A query or job you can run in a browser does not by itself give the collector access; grant it explicit permission to read, for example, the intended CloudWatch log groups.  Going deeper: repairing a collection cursor A cursor records where collection last stopped. Before repairing one, stop concurrent collection, record the current cursor and back up the database. Rewinding can process records twice; advancing can skip history. Resume with a bounded run and verify timestamps and counts. Each adapter keeps its own cursor format, so there is no single reset command to apply across adapters. ',
