    title: 'Map structured fields from agent events',
    book: 'Using KEEN',
    url: 'using-structured-fields.html#structured-evidence-fields',
    text: " Agent and OTLP events carry structured fields such as the process name, the HTTP status or the log file. This chapter shows how to write rules against those fields, which operator each situation needs, and two worked examples for nginx logs.  In Admin → Sources & Evidence Mapping , select New mapping and KEEN Agent / OTLP logs . Choose a sample event, then add field conditions. Field labels follow the JSON keys you see on the event: fields → process.name means the literal process.name key inside fields .  Evidence Condition All package activity Action regex ^package\\. Agent's own journal messages fields → SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER equals keen-agent , or use fields → _SYSTEMD_UNIT equals keen-agent.service in a separate rule. Installed packages Action package.status and fields → package_status equals installed . SSH session messages fields → SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER equals the observed program, such as sshd-session . Inspect the message before choosing a more specific condition. Cron invocations When emitted by the parser, action scheduled_job.started ; optionally constrain fields → job.command .  Going deeper: which “source” is which The outer KEEN source is keen-agent . Inside the normalised payload, source keeps the configured agent input, such as system or dpkg , and System identifies the registered agent. The original JSON is your guide to the values available to a rule. ",
