    text: ' KEEN keeps one set of risk scenarios behind the risk editor, the register and the heatmap. A risk can carry confidentiality, integrity and availability (CIA) associations, an asset, categories, an owner, treatment information and controls from any relevant framework.  In Risks , describe the threat and its business context, select or create the asset, set the CIA associations and assign an owner. Keep each scenario specific enough to support a treatment decision; several indistinguishable risks for the same situation help nobody.  Rate the inherent likelihood and impact first, then the residual ratings after the intended treatment. Record the treatment strategy, status, plan and due date so a lower residual score comes with its explanation.  Score Default band 1–4 Low 5–9 Moderate 10–16 High 17–25 Critical  Threshold changes apply across the whole register, so agree the scoring method before changing them. An unrated risk is different from a low risk: complete the ratings before you prioritise.  Use the reusable risk library  The Reusable risk library: scenarios with example ratings that you adapt into your own register with Use template.  Open the Risk register and find the Reusable risk library . Choose a scenario to start a new assessment: KEEN fills in the description, CIA tags, treatment guidance and any example assessment fields. Review the suggested asset and example ratings, set the real owner and treatment, and save the risk as your own.  To contribute a scenario of your own, select a risk in the register and choose Save scenario to library . Use a general name and remove customer-specific or personal details first. Reuse always creates an independent assessment.  Going deeper: library templates and KEEN-AF controls The supplied library integrates with the KEEN Assurance Framework. Templates can suggest KEEN-AF control references; when you create the risk, valid remaining references are linked under KEEN-AF:1.0 even if another framework is selected. Links for other frameworks stay separately manageable. Check implementation evidence for each suggested control.  Risk register exchange and the Mitigator  Export CSV gives you the register for an external review and the current column headers for an import. Import creates new scenarios only; edit existing records in the risk editor.  The KEEN Mitigator suggests controls from scenario categories, CIA associations and other assessment inputs. Use its suggestions alongside the library and your own analysis, and remove relationships that do not fit.  Going deeper: import limits An import accepts up to 1,000 rows and 2 MB, uses the intended framework for control references, and rejects the whole file if any row fails validation. Review ownership, scores and control references before importing, since re-importing the same file duplicates risks. ',
