    title: 'Assess a risk through to treatment',
    book: 'Using KEEN',
    url: 'using-risks.html#risk-assessment',
    text: ' The risk register: a 5×5 heatmap of inherent or residual ratings beside the register, with treatment decisions and linked controls for each scenario.  Open Risks to create a scenario, or choose Use template in the reusable library. Describe the threat, the affected asset and the business consequence. “Cyber risk” on its own is a label, not an assessment. Select the asset, category and subcategory, the CIA associations and an owner. CIA describes the effects of a scenario; the 1–5 ratings assess likelihood and impact. Rate inherent likelihood and impact before treatment, and record the basis. Likelihood 4 and impact 5 give an inherent score of 20. Choose the relevant controls and a treatment strategy. In the register, record the treatment status, plan and due date, and give the owner a concrete action with completion evidence to obtain. Set residual likelihood and impact for the position after treatment; 2 × 3 gives 6. Explain why the treatment changes the risk and link evidence of completed treatment. Review the saved risk in the register and the heatmap. Follow its controls and compare the supporting evidence with the treatment claim.  Rate / treat: inherent and residual likelihood and impact, the treatment decision, its status and due date, and the plan.  Review assessments when assets, threats, vendors, controls or the organisation change.  Reusable scenarios and CSV exchange  A template supplies a starting description, CIA tags, treatment guidance and any example ratings; assess the actual context rather than accepting them. Save scenario to library contributes a generalised scenario from a register risk, and assessments stay independent when their template is later edited or deleted.  For a CSV exchange, export first to get the headers, then import to create new scenarios. Check identifiers, scores, owner details and the framework used for control references before you import. ',
