    text: ' The KEEN Mitigator turns a plain-language concern into suggestions you can review. Describe the worry, name the asset, party or process, pick the category, subcategory, CIA associations and impact flags, and choose Analyse scenario . The mascot turns while the analysis runs; results take a few seconds on a large catalogue.  Mitigator results: the signals it matched, existing risks to review first, and library scenarios with their suggested controls.  Describe the concern in plain language, name the affected asset and weight the CIA dimensions before analysing.  Results can include suggested controls, similar risks already in your register, reusable templates, PESTLE(E) issues and interested parties. Matching terms and relevance indicators explain why each candidate appears. Nothing is saved until you review it in the normal editor.  Read the detected signals and check they describe your concern. A keyword match can still be irrelevant, or refer to something already prevented. Look at the library candidates and their control references. Review this template opens the risk editor so you can check the description and example ratings before saving. Review the suggested controls and remove unsuitable ones. Out-of-scope controls are labelled and keep their current applicability until you edit it. Check the existing matching risks before creating another assessment. Review PESTLE(E) and Interested Party candidates separately. Choose Create risk register entry to save your scenario with the selected controls, or follow a template. Set likelihood and impact yourself and complete the owner and treatment.  Try it with “Our staff use unapproved AI tools”, “Supplier contracts do not protect personal data”, or “We cannot restore our backups after ransomware”. If the matches are too broad, add context.  Going deeper: how the matching works Analysis runs inside KEEN with deterministic, explainable rules and no external AI service. It normalises words and common variants, recognises configured keywords and synonyms, and ranks relevant catalogue entries. It can match negated statements, so confirm each proposed risk against the actual scenario. In KEEN-AF:1.0 scope the wizard also searches the reusable library, shows the matching terms, and uses matching templates’ KEEN-AF control references to strengthen its control recommendations, including the extended library’s AI risks such as prompt injection, model extraction, unapproved AI tools and excessive agent permissions. Other framework scopes keep framework-specific suggestions without importing KEEN-AF library links. Edits to the library and control catalogue are read on the next analysis. ',
