    text: ' SWOT is where you write down what helps you, what holds you back, what you could pursue and what could go wrong, and then plan a response to each. This chapter takes you from one item to a whole analysis you can filter, review, export and audit.  The read-only SWOT quadrants on the ISMS page, with significance, due date and status for each item.  Open ISMS → SWOT analysis . The SWOT tab on the ISMS page shows read-only quadrants; Edit SWOT analysis opens the planning workspace at /swot.html .  Quadrant What to consider Example response Strength An existing capability that supports the organisation, such as an experienced incident response team. Maintain skills through exercises and share expertise. Weakness An internal limitation, such as dependence on one person for recovery procedures. Document the procedure and train a second operator. Opportunity A favourable possibility, such as automating repeated assurance checks. Pilot automation and evaluate coverage and time saved. Threat A condition that could undermine an outcome, such as widespread staff absence. Review continuity arrangements and test critical cover.  How SWOT relates to risks and objectives. SWOT captures context and choices. Risks hold assessed scenarios with likelihood, impact and treatment. Objectives hold measurable outcomes and the work to reach them. Link the three so a reviewer can follow an issue to an action and its evaluation. ',
