    text: ' A complete backup covers PostgreSQL, the referenced artefacts, the encryption material, the required configuration and secrets, and the release identifiers they belong to. Record backup success and restore-test success separately: only the second proves you can recover.  Take a database dump to a protected destination, following the administrator guide. Inventory the matching S3 versions or local artefacts and keep the keys needed to decrypt them. Restore into an isolated, empty recovery environment with outbound integrations and schedules disabled, so a rehearsal cannot notify anyone or ingest on behalf of the live service. Use a compatible application version and check sign-in, a framework, a risk, a document version, an evidence mapping and an artefact download, with representative checksums. Rehearse migrations against the copy and record the schema revisions before and after. Keep the original deployment intact until recovery is verified and cutover is deliberate. Rolling back only a container is safe only if the older code supports the migrated schema.  Going deeper: retention is not deletion Object retention and eventual deletion are different policies. Include noncurrent versions, legal holds and backups in the lifecycle plan. ',
