    url: 'administrator-redmine.html#redmine-3',
    text: ' Choose Create mapping , source redmine . Leave collection selection broad, or select a configured project collection, and use a real event or structured conditions to refine it.  Event action Meaning issue.snapshot Current issue state when captured issue.comment Journal with comment text, possibly including field changes issue.updated Journal containing field changes without comment text  All use outcome info : closing a ticket does not automatically prove a security control succeeded. The snapshot actor is unset because the original author may not be the person who made the latest change; the journal actor identifies the journal author, and edits can also expose journal.updated_by .  Example: source redmine , action issue.comment , project identifier equals infrastructure , and journal notes contains patching completed . Name the mapping “Documented patch deployment and review”, then choose the appropriate patch-management controls.  Going deeper: useful JSON paths and historical journals fields["project.identifier"] , fields["project.id"] , fields["project.name"] fields["issue.id"] , fields["issue.subject"] fields["issue.status.name"] , fields["issue.tracker.name"] fields["issue.priority.name"] , fields["issue.assigned_to.name"] fields["issue.custom_field.12"] (actual Redmine custom-field ID) fields["journal.notes"] , fields["journal.private_notes"] fields["change.attr.status_id.changed"] equals true fields["change.attr.status_id.new_value"] equals your resolved status ID For field-only journals use issue.updated . To include both comments and changes, leave action empty and require the relevant change.*.changed field. KEEN\'s structured fields are ANDed, and status IDs do not have identical meanings across installations. issue.* is the issue state at capture time, including on historical journal events; the journal\'s details / change.* fields describe its historical change, so inspect them when mapping a historical transition. Historical comments imported in one scan may share the current issue status, which is not the status at the time each comment was written. Events retain collection provenance and a link back to the Redmine issue or journal. They use the standard KEEN redaction, artifact storage, SHA-256 and framework-rule pipeline, including S3 or immutable retention when configured. Stable content identities suppress repeated unchanged records; edited records produce new evidence versions and earlier artifacts remain. Switching collection selectors does not rewrite old provenance, so use structured project fields to map historical events across such a change. ',
