    title: 'Operation and limits',
    book: 'Administering KEEN',
    url: 'administrator-riskledger.html#riskledger-operation',
    text: ' Scheduled and manual runs share a lock, and pause/resume is available with the other source controls. Each run scans current API records and unchanged snapshots are deduplicated. The last successful run is recorded only after collection completes; a failed run keeps completed snapshots and reports a partial result, and the next run scans again.  Going deeper: bounds, failures and retention Pages, response sizes, request timeouts and run duration are bounded. HTTP 429 or other API failures end the run without an immediate retry loop; review the error and wait for the next scheduled run or run it manually later. For large accounts, adjust page and run limits deliberately, because repeated early failures can prevent reaching later records until the underlying problem is resolved. Standard KEEN redaction applies before persistence, including native imported text. Snapshot artefacts use the configured local or S3 storage and existing evidence-retention rules. Native vendors, assets and risks have their own lifecycle; purging events does not delete these ISMS records.  API reference: Risk Ledger API , suppliers , risks and control lookup . ',
