    title: 'Evidence and framework mapping',
    book: 'Administering KEEN',
    url: 'administrator-agents.html#agent-evidence-and-framework-mapping',
    text: ' The server assigns source=keen-agent and binds system to the administrator-registered name; client-supplied host names remain descriptive attributes. The normalised payload includes source (the configured log source), action , outcome , actor , raw , fields and the stable event ID, and parsed fields are available as structured-field conditions in the rule editor.  Use Admin → Evidence definitions to create rules against these fields for your own framework and controls. Useful criteria include source=keen-agent , action=package.upgrade , action=auditd.SYSCALL or action=http.request with outcome=failure . Rules are not installed automatically for an assumed framework version, so preview them in the rule editor.  Going deeper: what an agent record proves The artifact contains the normalised, redacted record; raw pointers include agent ID, event ID, receive time and the canonical received-record digest. A recorded update demonstrates an observed maintenance action; a compliance claim about patch timeliness additionally needs asset scope, applicable requirements, deadlines and measurements. Host logs and heartbeats are claims from a credentialed collector, and a compromised host can falsify them, so preserve independent corroboration where required. ',
