    url: 'administrator-maintenance.html#9-upgrade-safely',
    text: ' Upgrades, backups and diagnosis follow the same principle: know your rollback point before you change anything, and check one representative record of each kind afterwards.  Record the current image versions, database revision and configuration. Take a database backup and protect the corresponding artefacts, encryption material and environment secrets. Restore that backup in a test environment and rehearse the upgrade there first. Quiesce ingestion and writes for the window where a consistent rollback point is required. Build or pull the reviewed release, run alembic upgrade head once, start the API/UI and worker, and start beat after checking the migrations. Verify sign-in, a framework node, an existing risk, a document version, a mapped event and an artefact download.  Rolling back. A container rollback is safe only if the earlier application understands the migrated schema. Otherwise restore the pre-upgrade database and compatible object state into an isolated recovery environment. Do not run an Alembic downgrade against production without a reviewed data-loss analysis.  Going deeper: routine Compose commands For routine service inspection use docker compose ps and scoped logs. docker compose stop preserves volumes; docker compose down -v removes them and is destructive, so use stop/start for a routine restart. Avoid printing the fully resolved Compose configuration into shared logs because it can contain secrets. ',
