    text: " Password verification never produces a session on its own when MFA is enrolled or required. It produces a short-lived pending challenge, and only a verified second factor turns that into an application session.  The pending challenge lasts five minutes and is opaque: the browser receives an HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict cookie, and the database stores the token hash, user, purpose, stage, credential-version snapshot, password-hash digest and expiry. It never enters the application-session namespace and cannot authorise API access or WebSocket handshakes. Successful verification consumes it before a fresh local session is issued.  Sessions carry the user's MFA version and verification state; password and factor changes invalidate older local sessions at subsequent authentication checks, while SSO sessions are governed separately. Account and operator changes write MFA audit entries without storing submitted codes or secrets.  Going deeper: factor management, WebAuthn and tests Factor management starts with fresh password verification and an existing factor when enrolled. All MFA POSTs enforce the configured exact Origin, verification rate limits fail closed when Valkey is unavailable, database row locks serialise factor changes and recovery-code consumption per user, and TOTP verification accepts a narrow clock window and rejects a previously accepted time step. WebAuthn registration and authentication use the maintained Python webauthn verifier, checking challenge, RP ID, origin, user presence, signatures and counters, and rejecting cross-origin ceremonies and mismatched user handles. User verification is preferred: the local password supplies the first factor, and a security key without a biometric or PIN can supply the second. Attestation is not used to restrict hardware vendors. The database stores user MFA state, encrypted TOTP secrets, WebAuthn credentials, recovery hashes and pending challenges. tests/test_local_mfa.py exercises PostgreSQL persistence, TOTP and recovery replay, origin and expiry handling, policy, session revocation and cryptographically signed WebAuthn ceremonies; hardware and browser interoperability belongs in deployment acceptance testing. MFA presentation selects a registered key by default and remembers a successful normal factor per account and browser. Verification completion finishes a login automatically, management ceremonies stay on the security settings screen, enrolment requires explicit recovery-code acknowledgement, and recovery codes are never remembered as the preferred method. ",
